<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Applies to every `dotnet test` in this repository, wired in by Directory.Build.props.

  TestSessionTimeout is here because a deadlocked test used to hang the suite
  indefinitely rather than fail it: nothing in dotnet test, the CI job or the local
  gate bounds a test run, so a stuck reader waits for as long as anybody lets it.
  A run past this bound is not a slow machine, it is a hang, and a hang that fails is
  worth more than one nobody can interrupt.

  Forty-five minutes is three times the slowest session, which is
  Sts2PilotTrainer.Arbiter.Tests at 14m06s on a machine with the game prepared; the
  other three projects together are under seven minutes and the game-free CI job is
  seconds. The bound is per session rather than per solution, so the slowest session is
  what it has to clear.

  It was fifteen minutes, and this comment used to justify that as three times a whole
  suite measured at 4m57s. The suite grew and the margin did not: by the time it was
  noticed the Arbiter session alone was within a minute of the bound, so an ordinary run
  aborted whenever anything else was running on the machine - and an aborted run still
  prints "Passed!" with a smaller total, because the tests it never reached are not
  failures. Only the exit code and a "Test Run Aborted" line say otherwise. Three runs
  were read as green here before that was spotted.

  So if this needs raising again, raise it and re-measure the number above rather than
  removing it. A bound that a healthy run can hit is worse than no bound, because it
  fails in the one direction nobody checks.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>2700000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
